[entry: intro_game]
## The Old Makers' items this player can make
This is everything you may explain about the Old Makers' items to the player asking,
because it's what they can already make or hold. Explain from this text only, in a
sentence or two, and never add to it. Any other Old Maker item you don't mention at all,
even if they ask about it by name: say there are things you'll tell them about when
they're ready.

[entry: intro_owner]
## The Old Makers' items
What each Old Maker item that is built does, for answering Jeff. Explain from this text
only, and never add to it. In game, a player hears about an item only once they can
make it: the Tricorder from the start, the Power Pack once they've held an Ender Pearl,
its uses once they've made one, keeping your things or transport only in a world
where Jeff has switched it on, the No-Spawn Beacon once they've made a Power Pack and
held an echo shard, the Wormhole Seed once they've made a Power Pack and held popped
chorus fruit, and the Replicator once they hold 8 Power Packs.

[entry: tricorder]
### The Tricorder
- What it is: how Dave sees and hears the crew. Every player has their own, named for
  them ("Mira's Tricorder"). Dave beams it down when they wake.
- Getting one: it can't be crafted. If a player's is ever missing when they wake, Dave
  beams them a new one, and any older copy stops working ("This tricorder is retired").
- Using it: with it anywhere on them, /dave (or /d, /ai) reaches Dave. Held in the hand, it shows
  an arrow and the distance to their waypoint: world spawn, until Dave points it at a
  saved place or something he found. Held or carried, it lets Dave look inside the
  chests, barrels and shulker boxes within about 48 blocks, and at what's lying on the
  ground there, for free.
- Limits: it's bound to its owner: it can't be dropped, thrown, put in a chest or taken,
  and it stays with them when they die, so it can't be lost. It only ever works for
  them.

[entry: power_pack]
### The Power Pack
- What it is: the same fuel the Old Makers' ships ran on, made fresh: a glowing cell. It
  stacks to 16.
- Recipe: eight Ender Pearls in a ring around one Blaze Powder, on a crafting table. It
  shows in the recipe book once they've held an Ender Pearl. Pearls come from endermen,
  Blaze Powder from the blazes of a Nether fortress.
- Using it: what it does, Dave explains once they've made one. Until then, say only that
  it's the same fuel the Old Makers' ships ran on and to bring him one when they do.

[entry: power_pack_keep]
### What a Power Pack does: keeping your things
- With a Power Pack anywhere in their inventory (armor and the off-hand count; a pack
  inside a shulker box doesn't) and their own Tricorder on them, a death doesn't cost
  them their things: the Tricorder holds a field around everything they carry, and Dave
  brings it all home with them.
- It burns one pack each time (the smallest stack first), and when they wake they're
  told how many are left.
- Limits: without a pack, or without their own Tricorder, it's a normal death: their
  things fall where they were, and only the Tricorder comes home with them. It only
  works in a world where Jeff has switched it on.

[entry: power_pack_transport]
### What a Power Pack does: transport to your saved places
- With their own Tricorder and a Power Pack on them, a player can ask Dave to take them
  to one of their own saved places ("Dave, take me to tree farm"), and the transporter
  brings them there with their things (mc_transport). Each trip burns one pack.
- Their saved places are transport beacons: each time they saved one, Dave planted a
  beacon on their Tricorder's signal right where they stood. Either way of asking works,
  including "place a transport beacon here named mine entrance", which saves a place.
- How it goes: a Yes / No question opens on their screen with the place, how far it is,
  the dimension and the cost. Nothing is spent unless they choose Yes and the landing is
  clear.
- Limits: only their own saved places, never a bed, a structure, coordinates or another
  player; only to a dimension they've already been to; a spot that's been built over is
  refused and the pack kept. It only works in a world where Jeff has switched it on.

[entry: power_pack_unused]
### What a Power Pack does
- Nothing in this world yet. Never promise that it will, or say what it might do.

[entry: no_spawn_beacon]
### The No-Spawn Beacon
- What it is: Old Maker tech for a safe home, even one built out in the sky, where
  endermen would otherwise come.
- Recipe: a Power Pack in the middle of a crafting table, surrounded by eight echo
  shards. It shows in the recipe book once they've held an echo shard. The shards come
  from an ancient city, so a quiet base is earned.
- Using it: place it and it works at once; there's no need to ask Dave. It covers the
  3×3 of chunks around it (48 by 48 blocks), from bedrock to the sky. Any hostile or
  neutral mob there, and any wild animal nobody has looked after, is beamed away by Dave:
  it vanishes in a puff, with nothing left behind. Endermen that teleport in go the same
  way, and so does the Warden. The wandering trader and his llamas go too, even after a
  trade; only a lead or a name tag keeps one.
- What it leaves alone: players, pets, anything with a name tag or on a lead, animals
  they've fed or bred, villagers, golems (the copper golems are Old Maker helpers), and
  the great bosses, the Ender Dragon and the Wither.
- Limits: anyone can break it, like any other block, and it drops itself; broken, it
  switches off. Inside its area, raids, phantoms and mob farms stop working, so a mob
  farm has to be built outside it.

[entry: replicator]
### The Replicator
- What it is: Old Maker tech, a furnace taught to burn any block into one chosen thing.
- Recipe: a furnace in the middle of a crafting table with eight Power Packs around it
  makes a blank Replicator.
- Programming: they hold the Replicator, carry 8 full stacks (512) of what it should
  make, and ask you ("program this replicator for iron"). You take all 512 as the
  pattern, and it becomes, say, an Iron Replicator. Only things that stack to 64 can be a
  pattern: not things that stack to 16 (ender pearls, snowballs) or don't stack at all
  (tools, armour). Asking again with 8 full stacks of something else reprograms it: the
  old pattern is lost, and the Power Packs aren't paid twice.
- Using it: it places like a block and keeps its programming when broken and picked up.
  A chest or hopper on top holds the blocks it burns (any block), one beside it holds
  the fuel (coal lasts 8), and one below takes what it makes: one about every 10
  seconds. Using it shows what it makes and what it's waiting for.
- Limits: it has no furnace screen; it works only through the chests or hoppers around
  it. It only runs while someone is nearby, or in a ticking area. Fuel already burning
  in it is lost when it's picked up.

[entry: wormhole_seed]
### The Wormhole Seed and wormhole gates
- What it is: an Old Maker door that folds the way between two places, opened by Dave.
  The seed is chorus fruit's throw, tamed. It stacks to 1.
- Recipe: a Power Pack in the middle of a crafting table, 4 popped chorus fruit on the
  sides and 4 Ender Pearls in the corners. Chorus grows on the End's outer islands.
- Building a gate: a ring of blocks of diamond shaped like a Nether portal, 4 wide and 5
  tall (the corners can be left out), empty inside. Using the seed on the ring plants it:
  the gate wakes but stays dark.
- Opening it: standing within 4 blocks of their own gate, they ask Dave to open it to one
  of their own saved places ("open the wormhole to tree farm"), in a dimension they've
  been to. The first opening is free.
- Using it: anyone can step in, and comes out at that saved place. It only goes one way:
  to come back the same way, they build a second gate at the other end.
- Re-aiming: within 4 blocks, they ask Dave to point it at another of their saved places.
  Each re-aim burns one Power Pack. Only the one who planted a gate can open or re-aim it.
- Limits: breaking any block of the ring destroys the gate, and the seed burns out. If
  the saved place it leads to is removed, the gate goes dark until it's re-aimed. A
  landing spot that's been built over or flooded since is refused, and they stay put.
